  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Dry and notably less humid finish to the weekend, \nfollowed by a return to a somewhat more active pattern from late \ntonight through the first half of the new work week.\n\nOther than some areas of low clouds and patchy fog to start the day, \nexpect a dry and pleasant finish to the weekend, with highs \nclimbing to within a few degrees of seasonal norms. \n\nA system moving by to the north area will pump in another surge of \ndeeper moisture that will accompany the arrival of a warm \nfront/strong LLJ segment later tonight. This will bring the next \nchance for showers and storms into western NY late tonight, shifting \neast across the remainder of the region through early Monday \nafternoon. As the system moves northeast of the area later in the \nday, a trailing cold front will be forced south across western and \nnorth-central NY Monday afternoon and evening. A moist airmass will \nbe in place within the warm sector ahead of the boundary, with PWAT \nvalues in the 1.5\"-1.75\" range. This will contribute to at least \nmoderate diurnal instability to aid the development of scattered \nafternoon and evening thunderstorms. There will be the potential for \nlocally heavy rainfall within the moist airmass, for which WPC has a \nSlight Risk of Excessive Rainfall (Level 2 out of 4) across portions \nof the Southern Tier, with a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall \n(Level 1 out of 4) across the remainder of the region. There will \nalso be the threat of severe weather, mainly across the western \nSouthern Tier where moderate shear profiles will be in place, \nhowever instability may not be sufficient to sustain more robust \nconvection. Nonetheless, potential for a stronger storm or two will \nexist, with the main threat being strong winds. SPC continues with a \nMarginal Risk of Severe Weather (Level 1 out of 5) across the \nwestern Southern Tier. The greater severe risk remains to our \nsouthwest at this time.\n\nThe cold front will stall just to our south Monday night, then \nlinger as a stationary boundary through midweek. Much drier air will \nreside to the north of the boundary Tuesday, while drifting ever so \nslowly northward toward the NY/PA line. This will keep the better \nthreat of showers and storms going across the Southern Tier closer \nto the front, while the northern half of the area stays mainly dry. \nThat said, with general troughiness and associated cool pool aloft, \ncan't rule out a few showers/isolated storm Tuesday afternoon \nanywhere inland from the lakes owed to surface heating. A potent mid \nlevel shortwave and associated surface low is then progged to cross \nthe lower Great Lakes region Wednesday, bringing a renewed threat of \nshowers and storms midweek.",
